  "text": "Ruth rewrote her whole story in a barley field. Widowed, broke, and foreign. Her mother in law Naomi told her twice to go home to Moab, and Ruth said no. Whither thou goest, I will go. Thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God. Then they walk into Bethlehem with nothing, right at the start of barley harvest. And Ruth goes to work. The law said harvesters could not strip a field clean, so the poor were allowed to follow the reapers and pick up what fell. That is what she does. She asks to glean, and the text says her hap was to light on a field belonging to Boaz, a relative of her late husband's family. Her happenstance. Sure. Boaz notices her, tells his men not to touch her, tells her to drink their water, and quietly orders them to drop extra grain where she will find it. She goes home with about an ephah of barley. A few chapters later, Boaz redeems the family line at the town gate, and Ruth has a son named Obed. Obed's grandson is King David. Loyalty is a flex.",
